\name{readers_identify_many}
\alias{readers_identify_many}
\title{Identify many files in one bridge call}
\usage{
readers_identify_many(paths, threads = 1, on_error = "fail", timeout = 60)
}
\arguments{
\item{paths}{A character vector of paths. A Bruker \code{.d} directory is accepted.}

\item{threads}{Paths identified at once. \code{1}, the default, or \code{-1} for one per core. The answer
  does not depend on it.}

\item{on_error}{\code{"fail"}, the default, stops at the first path that cannot be identified.
  \code{"skip"} records why in that path's row and identifies the rest.}

\item{timeout}{Seconds to allow, or \code{NULL} to wait indefinitely.
}

}
\description{
Say what kind of file a path is - the mzLib file type, its extension, its reader and the cross-format views it offers - without parsing its contents.

The many-files form of \code{\link{readers_identify}}: one bridge process resolves every path, \code{threads}
at a time, and answers in input order.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_identify_batch}. \code{files} is a data.frame with one row per path, in input
  order: \code{path}, \code{file_type}, \code{extension}, \code{reader}, a \code{views} list column, \code{is_quantifiable},
  and \code{error_kind}, \code{error_type} and \code{error_message}, which are \code{NA} for a path that was
  identified. \code{file_count}, \code{read_count} and \code{failed_count} count paths.

}
